description.narrative:
The project “Promoting green transformation and sustainable livelihoods for ethnic minority women through cinnamon value chains in Nghia Do and Bao Ha communes, Lao Cai province” was developed to promote green transformation, improve livelihoods and empower the economy of ethnic minority women in Bao Yen district – a key cinnamon area of ​​Lao Cai province. This area has more than 60% of the population being ethnic minorities, whose income depends heavily on cinnamon trees, but is facing many challenges:climate change, outdated drying and processing techniques, high costs, large post-harvest losses and unstable markets. Women – the labor group with the highest proportion in the cinnamon value chain – still mainly work in heavy, low-value stages, with little access to markets, technology and leadership positions in cooperatives/groups. Based on this urgent need, the initiative proposes a model combining infrared drying technology using biomass gasification stoves, along with solutions to enhance management capacity and market connection forcooperatives/groups led by women. The new technology helps save 50% of firewood, utilizes by-products (branches, leaves, bark), reduces dependence on weather and reduces 20% loss due to mold, while creating a safe working environment suitable for female workers. Improving cinnamon quality and developing new products helps increase product value by 10-15%, directly contributing to the goal of increasing women's income by at least 20%.

In addition to technological innovation, the initiative focuses on building capacity forfemale members and leaders in cooperatives/groups, through training in management, business planning, product development, marketing and decision-making. In parallel, the project implements market connection activities with cinnamon processing and distribution enterprises in and outside the province, establishes a stable consumption mechanism and builds a brand identity system, QR codes for traceability of key products.

* The main beneficiaries include:
300 ethnic minority women who grow cinnamon have increased income and improved working conditions;
60 female members of the cooperative are trained in management, business and product development;
10 female cooperative leaders have enhanced management skills and lead green transformation models;
At least 3 MSME enterprises are connected to a stable source of cinnamon, improving quality and traceability.

* The initiative was implemented for 15 months in two communes of Nghia Do and Bao Ha, with a total budget of 1,636,500,060 VND sponsored by Stiching Oxfam Novib. With its practicality andsuitability with the sustainable agricultural development planning and OCOP program of Lao Cai province, the initiative has high potential to be replicated as a women-led green transformation model in the northern mountainous region.
